diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF routing (ospfd) on r7. Mechanism: When r7’s OSPF process stops, it withdraws its LSAs; upstream routers (including r1) flush 17.x.x.x loopbacks and the connected aggregates 67.0.0.0/8 and 70.0.0.0/8 from the OSPF database, so r1’s routing view of the “R7 region” goes empty. Localization: r7’s OSPF adjacency on eth0 (67.1.1.2) to neighbor 67.1.1.1 (Router-ID 16.1.4.1) in area 1. Evidence shows a recent flap and recovery: on r7, “show ip ospf neighbor” reports Full/Backup with Up Time ~50s; r7’s ospfd process start time is Sat Aug 8 08:52:32 (ps output), matching that flap. On r1, the OSPF routes for 67.0.0.0/8 and 70.0.0.0/8, and 17.1.x.1/32 all reappeared only ~40–50 seconds ago (route ages), confirming they had been missing and were just relearned. Root cause: r7’s ospfd transiently died/stopped and was restarted (likely by watchfrr), temporarily removing R7’s prefixes from the network. Corrective fix: Service is currently restored—no immediate action required. To prevent recurrence, keep watchfrr supervising ospfd (it is running) and enable persistent logging/coredumps to capture future crashes. Consider upgrading FRR or reviewing r7’s OSPF config (it’s standard: area 1 on 67/8, 70/8, and 17.1.x.0/24s) and enabling BFD for faster failure detection. If the flap repeats, collect /var/log/frr/frr.log and core for analysis and redeploy ospfd via watchfrr. === END ===